.TH chmod "" "Command"
.SH NAME
chmod \- change permissions of a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBchmod \fImode file ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
chmod -- change permissions of a file
Usage:	chmod mode file ...
Mode may be octal or a comma-separated symbolic list:
	[which]howperm...[,...]
'which':
	a	User, group, and other permissions
	g	Group permissions
	o	Other permissions
	u	User permissions
Missing 'which' implies 'a'; 'g', 'o', and 'u' may be combined.
'how':
	=	Set permissions
	+	Add permissions
	-	Take away permissions
'perm':
	g	Current group permissions
	o	Current other permissions
	r	Read
	s	Setuid on execution
	t	Sticky bit (save text)
	u	Current user permissions
	w	Write
	x	Execute
.HE
.B chmod
changes the access permissions of each
.I file
to
.IR mode .
.PP
If
.I mode
begins with a digit, it is read as an octal number and becomes the
new mode directly.
.PP
Otherwise
.I mode
is a comma-separated list of one or more clauses of the form
.RS
.B [\fIwhich\fB]\fIhow\fBperm\fR...
.RE
each of which changes the permission bits selected by
.I which
according to
.I how
using the values given by
.IR perm .
Several clauses may be joined with commas to change more than one
category in a single invocation, e.g.
.B u+w,go\-w
adds write permission for the owner and removes it for group and
other.
If
.I which
is omitted, it defaults to
.B a
adjusted by the file-creation mask (see
.BR umask() ).
.PP
.I which
selects one or more of:
.IP \fBu\fR
The file's owner permissions.
.IP \fBg\fR
The file's group permissions.
.IP \fBo\fR
The file's other permissions.
.IP \fBa\fR
All three of the above; this is the default if
.I which
is omitted.
.PP
.I how
is one of:
.IP \fB=\fR
Set the selected permissions to
.IR perm .
.IP \fB+\fR
Add the permissions named by
.I perm
to the selected permissions.
.IP \fB\-\fR
Remove the permissions named by
.I perm
from the selected permissions.
.PP
.I perm
is one or more of:
.IP \fBr\fR
Read.
.IP \fBw\fR
Write.
.IP \fBx\fR
Execute (search, for a directory).
.IP \fBs\fR
Set-user-id and set-group-id on execution.
.IP \fBt\fR
Sticky bit (save text image after execution).
.IP \fBu\fR
The file's current owner permissions, replicated onto the
selected
.IR which .
.IP \fBg\fR
The file's current group permissions, replicated onto the
selected
.IR which .
.IP \fBo\fR
The file's current other permissions, replicated onto the
selected
.IR which .
.PP
.I mode
may also be given entirely as an octal number, exactly as for the
traditional octal mode word.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chgrp ,
.BR chown ,
.B umask()
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B chmod
prints
.B "chmod: badly formed octal mode"
or
.B "chmod: badly formed symbolic mode"
and exits if
.I mode
cannot be parsed.
If a
.I file
cannot be changed, it prints the reason and exits with status 2
after processing the remaining files.
.SH NOTES
Setting the sticky bit
.RB ( t )
sets both the set-user-id and set-group-id bits together, since this
implementation does not distinguish them in the
.I perm
letter
.BR s .
A non-superuser who sets the sticky bit gets a one-time warning;
enforcement of the restriction is otherwise left to the
.B chmod()
system call.
